Which files contain Hermes-derived text
---------------------------------------

`compact-overrides.example.json`

    The `prompt_strings` section reproduces system-prompt text that is also
    present verbatim in Hermes Agent's own source
    (`agent/prompt_builder.py`). Nous Research's MIT notice above applies to
    that text. The `tool_descriptions` and `parameter_descriptions` sections are
    independent paraphrases written for this project.

`apply_compact.py` / `compact_ui.py`

    These scripts append a clearly-marked, self-contained block at the end of
    three Hermes Agent source files:

        <hermes-agent>/model_tools.py
        <hermes-agent>/agent/prompt_builder.py
        <hermes-agent>/agent/coding_context.py

    Running the tool therefore combines your local Hermes Agent checkout (MIT,
    Nous Research) with this project's code. The two licences are compatible —
    both MIT — and no copyleft obligation is created. Always keep your Hermes
    Agent checkout's own `LICENSE` file intact.

The user's own `overrides/` and `overrides.d/*.json` files are not part of this
distribution and are git-ignored.
